.TH FKCAT 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkcatfd, fkcatf, fkcat \- copy a file to a file descriptor or stream
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "ssize_t fkcatfd(const char *" path ", int " fd );
.BI "ssize_t fkcatf(const char *" path ", FILE *" f );
.PP
.BI "ssize_t fkcat(const char *" path );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkcatfd ()
copies the contents of the file named by
.I path
to the file descriptor
.IR fd .
.BR fkcatf ()
copies them to the stream
.IR f .
.BR fkcat ()
is a macro equivalent to
.IR fkcatfd(path,\~STDOUT_FILENO) .
.PP
No
.B fkstring
is built: the file is copied through a single 64\~KiB buffer
.RI ( _catbufsize ),
so memory use does not depend on its size.
Embedded NUL bytes are copied unchanged.
Reads interrupted by a signal
.RB ( EINTR )
are retried, and so are short writes to
.IR fd .
.PP
.BR fkcatf ()
writes with
.BR fwrite (3),
so its output follows anything already buffered in
.IR f ,
and may still be in that buffer when it returns; call
.BR fflush (3)
if that matters.
.BR fkcatfd ()
bypasses stdio.
Before mixing it with stdio output to the same descriptor, such as
.BR fkcat ()
after
.BR printf (3),
call
.I fflush(stdout)
or the output can appear out of order.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of bytes copied, or
.B SSIZE_MAX
if that is larger.
On error, these functions return \-1 with
.I errno
set.
Bytes copied before the error remain written.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I path
is NULL, or, for
.BR fkcatf (),
.I f
is NULL.
.TP
.B EISDIR
.I path
is a directory.
.PP
These functions can also fail with any error of
.BR open (2),
.BR read (2),
or, for
.BR fkcatfd (),
.BR write (2).
.PP
They do not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH NOTES
.I path
is opened with
.BR O_CLOEXEC .
.PP
Copying a file that never ends, such as
.IR /dev/zero ,
does not return unless a write fails, but, unlike
.BR fkslurp (3),
it does not exhaust memory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
if (fkcat("/etc/hostname") < 0)
    perror("/etc/hostname");
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkslurp (3),
.BR fkstrwrite (3)
